, 0.03466796875, 0.0243988037109375, 0.01134490966796875, -0.01435089111328125, -0.040863037109375, -0.0012807846069335938, 0.00812530517578125, 0.009307861328125, -0.01012420654296875, -0.00991058349609375, 0.005184173583984375, -0.02471923828125, -0.0151214599609375, -0.0037078857421875, 0.0077667236328125, 0.0408935546875, -0.01053619384765625, -0.00640869140625, -0.0174407958984375, -0.021331787109375, 0.00388336181640625, -0.025909423828125, 0.0169830322265625, -0.01195526123046875, 0.014923095703125, -0.0070648193359375, -0.03857421875, -0.0008554458618164062, 0.0014896392822265625, -0.0009260177612304688, -0.0300140380859375, -0.0123443603515625, 0.042633056640625, -0.017425537109375, 0.006378173828125, -0.023651123046875, -0.0117034912109375, 0.031494140625, -0.002880096435546875, 0.0245361328125, 0.01739501953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent studies have explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, with a particular focus on electrical circuits. Central to this approach is the transformation of the ordinary differential equations governing system behavior from the real domain into the Laplace domain. Achieving this requires a rigorously defined Laplace transform within the context of GA. In this work, we extend previous research that generalized the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras by providing a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebra. Our formulation and its associated properties are valid for geometric algebras with signatures up to and including 5."}}